  "notes": [
    "Phase 3 plan, decision 4 (Q3, Q6). The calc computes these fields for the character and the main skill; calc-parity (P3-5) compares the Complete ones with each field's rule and the ten breakdowns term by term.",
    "comparison: every field is exact for now (Q6 default). A field moves to abs or rel only with a written reason once a successor output shows where summation order changes the last bits (P3-5 on); no broad tolerance (ADR-006).",
    "applies: always, or only when the main skill is an attack or a spell (calc_survey::rules::Kind).",
    "Average hit: PoB has no top-level AverageHit for attacks (it is per hand); the hit DPS line names output.AverageDamage for both kinds (Modules/CalcOffence.lua, breakdown.TotalDPS), which is the average hit times the hit chance for attacks and the average hit for spells. The slice field is AverageDamage.",
    "Rate: TotalDPS multiplies AverageDamage by HitSpeed when the skill has one (for example Winter Orb's hit rate), else by Speed; HitSpeed is compared when the oracle has it.",
    "Breakdown decimals: each term is compared at the precision its line prints. Lua formats: %.2f and %.1f fixed decimals; %g 6 significant digits; concatenation (\"..\") prints the number as Lua's tostring (14 significant digits) of a value PoB has already cut to an integer with m_modf.",
    "graph (revision 2): the node of our provenance graph each term is compared with (calc-parity, P3-5): base, increased, more (the first node with that term below the field's node, nearest first, children in order), result (the field's value), step.<name> (a node the engine labels Term::Step(\"<name>\")), field:<id> (another slice field's value). A slots row is the node step.slot.<source> (PoB's source, lowercased, spaces as -, and .<sourceName> when PoB names one), its parts searched below it; row is that node's value. A term PoB prints and our graph lacks is a difference; an optional term our graph has but PoB does not print must be 1 at its decimals.",
    "Line formats (revision 2): calc-parity compares each term at the first conversion of its line: %g and %.Nf as Lua's string.format, .. as Lua's tostring (%.14g), number as the exact JSON number.",
    "contributions name the parts of our provenance graph each term sums or multiplies (P3-4): base (Base effects and the values the character, items, and gems give), increased (1 + the sum of Increased / 100), more (the product of More), result (the field's value)."
  ],
